\page selected_hpc_platforms

Selected HPC platforms and toolchains {#chapter:selected-HPC-platforms}
=====================================

Clang toolchain
---------------

The NVC++, Intel's OneAPI and AMD compilers build upon Clang. However,
they modify the baseline compiler. Therefore, they have separated
sections in this chapter (below). The present section focuses on the
baseline Clang compiler suite:

./configure CC=clang CXX=clang++ CXXFLAGS=\"-O3 -std=c++20 -mtune=native
-march=native\"  --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing
--enable-blockstructured

##### OpenMP

On Ubuntu and many other systems, clang is not shipped automatically
with OpenMP. Instead, you have to install the package `libomp-dev`.

./configure CC=clang CXX=clang++  CXXFLAGS=\"-O3 -std=c++20
-mtune=native -march=native -fopenmp\"  --enable-exahype
--enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured
 --with-multithreading=omp

Should you require OpenMP offloading, i.e. GPU, support, you have to add
the OpenMP target instructions:

./configure CC=clang CXX=clang++  CXXFLAGS=\"-O3 -std=c++20
-mtune=native -march=native -fopenmp\" -fopenmp-targets=nvptx64
 --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured
 --with-multithreading=omp

Intel OneAPI toolchain
----------------------

OneAPI is what we currently use on Hamilton 8, i.e. our AMD EPYC
testbeds. However, the modules there are built against old versions of
GCC which do not provide the C++ standard library that we need for .
Therefore, you have to manually load the newest GCC *after* you have
loaded the OneAPI module.

Before we start, you either load a fitting OneAPI module, or you set the
environment variables via Intel's script:

source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh

The `source` command is included within a proper module. The LLVM-based
Intel compiler is straightforward to use:

./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx  CXXFLAGS=\"-Ofast -std=c++20 -xAVX
-mtune=native -march=native\"  --with-multithreading=omp
--enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured

which sets the most aggressive generic optimisation.

##### OpenMP {#section:supercomputers:openmp}

For OpenMP, we usually use the Intel runtime:

./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx  CXXFLAGS=\"-Ofast -std=c++20 -fiopenmp
-xAVX -mtune=native -march=native\"  LDFLAGS=\"-fiopenmp\"
 --with-multithreading=omp --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing
--enable-blockstructured

There are many different ways to specify the GPU (backend), but we often
use the spir64 fat binary approach:

./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx  CXXFLAGS=\"-Ofast -std=c++20 -fiopenmp
-xAVX -mtune=native -march=native -fopenmp-targets=spir64\"
 LDFLAGS=\"-fiopenmp\"  --with-multithreading=omp --enable-exahype
--enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured  --with-gpu=omp

##### Intel's DPC++

To use the data-parallel compiler (and hence to gain SYCL support), you
have to change `CXX`

./configure CC=dpcpp CXX=dpcpp  CXXFLAGS=\"-Ofast -mtune=native
-march=native\"  LDFLAGS=\"-ltbb\"  --with-multithreading=tbb
--enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured
 --with-gpu=sycl

In line with previous discussions, we use `dpcpp` both as C and as C++
compiler. While the SYCL GPU port should, in theory, work with any
tasking front-end and, notably, with C++ tasking, I usually use the
Threading Building Blocks (TBB) in . Please note that you will have to
link against `libtbb.so` via `-ltbb` manually.

##### Intel MPI

Intel's `mpiicpc` wrapper serves both the legacy Intel compiler and the
new LLVM version. We recommend to switch to the new build:

before you invoke

./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx \... --with-mpi=mpiicpc

Obviously, you have to fill in the remaining flags that you need for
your build. Notably, do not forget to add a multithreading backend, as
 does not particularly like sole MPI builds without multithreading.

##### Intel OneAPI toolchain (ITAC)

For Intel's ITAC, we have to load the itac module and reconfigure. We
found many supercomputers to deploy itac to modules of its own, i.e. it
is not loaded by default.

./configure CXXFLAGS=\"-DLogService=ITACLogger  --with-toolchain=intel

switches the logging to ITAC and activates the Intel toolchain. Please
note that it depends on your log filter file (Chapter
[\[section:logging\]](#section:logging){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:logging"}) what data is piped into ITAC and which
data isn't.

NVIDIA NVC++ toolchain
----------------------

The NVIDIA toolchain requires us to make `CXX` and `CC` point to the C++
compiler, as both are passed the same arguments by configure, which are
actually only understood by the C++ version.

Some compute kernels are not available with the NVIDIA tools, as the
compiler is pretty picky when it decides which temporary variables it
might place on the call stack.

##### OpenMP and OpenMP offloading

NVIDIA's compiler does not support all of OpenMP. Therefore, its OpenMP
implementation is rather picky when it comes to copy constructors and
other C++ features.  has internal workarounds for all of these items,
i.e. the build should in principle succeed yet the code might be
slightly slower than the Intel or native Clang counterpart.

The OpenMP offloading requires us to specify both the GPU OpenMP target
and a corresponding CUDA

It is important to recognise that `–with-gpu=omp` activates the
offloading from 's point of view. However, as long as the NVIDIA
compiler is not told which device to use, it will give you

Found 0 devices

if you run the kernel benchmark from page .

AMD toolchain
-------------

For the AMD toolchain we need to load either ROCm or AOMP. Both modules
should have AMD's version of Clang. With the AMD toolchain we use AMD's
modified Clang. We again need to point `CXX` and `CC` to the Clang
compiler.

##### OpenMP {#openmp-1}

The Clang compiler does not support `omp_get_mapped_ptr` at the moment.
We have implemented a workaround `mk_omp_get_mapped_ptr` in
`src/tarch/multicore/omp/MkUtils.h` which does the same trick.

For the configuration with OpenMP GPU offloading, we again specify
`–with-gpu=omp` to make Peano GPU aware. Furthermore, we need to add AMD
specific offloading instructions:

./configure  --enable-exahype  --enable-loadbalancing
 --enable-blockstructured  --with-gpu=omp  --with-multithreading=omp
 CC=clang CXX=clang++  CXXFLAGS=\"-O3 -std=c++20 -fopenmp -O3 -std=c++20
--offload-arch=\<gpu-arch\>\"  LDFLAGS=\"-fopenmp
--offload-arch=\<gpu-arch\> -lstdc++fs\"

where `<gpu-arch>` is either `gfx906` (MI 50), `gfx908` (MI 100), or
`gfx90a` (MI 200 series).

Some particular supercomputer testbeds
--------------------------------------

### Hamilton 8

One Hamilton 8 (AMD EPYC), we prefer to use the OneAPI toolchain via the
pre-installed modules. It is important to load an appropriate GCC
backend, too.

The `oneapi` is built against a GCC version which is too old and lacks
important features within its standard library. Therefore, you have to
manuall load the latest `gcc`. This is usually prevented by the system
and leads to a conflict (so that you can't have multiple GCC versions
loaded). We therefore have to disable conflict checks a priori.

For MPI, I recommend to use IntelMPI:

### NCC

I recommend atm to use `gpu10`, `gpu11` or `gpu12`, i.e. the A100
machines. By default, I usually work with NVC++ on this cluster, but the
OneAPI software stack should work, too.

